-- pending: rank 2: a callback whose parameter is a computation (#161 target 8)
-- A callback that takes a callback (target 8 of #161; SPEC §22): `with_logger` makes a callback
-- that logs and passes it to its own callback `use`, which calls it twice.
--
-- Blocked by: `use`'s code is made where `main` writes it, and would have to treat its parameter
-- `log` as a computation parameter: take its code, environment and capabilities, and pass them
-- at each call. Two things are missing. The callback `with_logger` makes is known only in
-- `with_logger`'s copy: its row, its capabilities and the type of its environment (a type the
-- caller of `with_logger` cannot name: an existential, or a type exposed per copy, would). And its
-- code is generic in the lifetimes of its capabilities, so `use`'s code would take a `∀` type as
-- an argument of a call of a local code value: synthesized where it is bound, compared by `=`
-- with no α-conversion of lifetime binders (`Frontend/README.md`, Incompleteness 2). Functions over
-- computations at any order work when the inner computation is a function's parameter
-- (`corpus/cb-pass-on.bc`, `cb-policy.bc`).
--
-- Once it lands: (i32[7], i32[3]): `use` answers 7 and logs 1 and 2.
--
-- now: 23:57: an operation of `Log` is performed inside a closure or block that is not run in place, nor passed at a function's computation parameter (SPEC §22): it cannot reach its handler (a stored callback, R6 of decision 57, is not built)

effect Log { fn note(x: i32) }
handler tally(mut n: i32): Log = { note(x) = { n = n + x; () } }

fn with_logger(use: !(!(i32 => 1) => i32)) -> i32 = use({ x -> note(x) })

main -> i32 * i32 =
  let l = alloc(0);
  let r = with tally(&mut l) { with_logger({ log -> log(1); log(2); 7 }) };
  (r, free(l))
